+## 2026-08-17 — A2 PR-1: plain `yeet monitor`'s fail-fast watch binds to the superseded head
+
+- What was happening: twice in one session, `yeet publish --monitor` reported "monitor phase
+  failed" immediately after a green proof and successful push, because
+  `gh pr checks --watch --fail-fast` started during the old-head→new-head transition and
+  tripped on the *superseded* head's terminal Coverage Regression red before the new head's
+  checks registered. Both times every check on the actually-pushed head was pending; the
+  verdict and the "rerun one job" guidance pointed at a run that no longer mattered.
+- Evidence: pushes ef60da45f8 and 1099b4cd09 on PR #759; in both cases
+  `gh pr view --json headRefOid` + `gh pr checks` showed all-pending on the new head while
+  the monitor phase exited red citing the prior run (32066802505 / 32077705569).
+- What would have prevented it: the monitor phase should resolve the PR head SHA first and
+  gate the watch on checks *for that SHA* (the A1 watch stream already carries a per-head
+  registration budget — `advanceYeetWatchTick` — the plain monitor's blocking watch predates
+  it). Until then the operator cost is a spurious red verdict plus one manual re-arm per push.
+
+## 2026-08-17 — the success-exit hang recurred on the hosted Docgen lane (post-#748)
+
+- What was happening: PR #759's Docgen check ran 54 minutes before the operator cancelled it.
+  The log tail shows the lane had already *succeeded*: turbo docgen exited 0 after 1m51s
+  ("✓ Docs generation succeeded!", 31/31 packages), the wrapper printed
+  "docgen:local: aggregating 31 package(s) into docs/generated" at 23:39, and the matching
+  "aggregated 31 package(s) in …" line (locally ~3s later) never appeared — ~51 minutes of
+  silence until cancellation, when the runner reaped five orphaned bun processes
+  (pids 5110/5111/5255-5257) that were holding the step's stdio pipes.
+- Why it matters: this is the third recurrence of the success-exit hang class, on a third
+  lane — #718's process-group reap did not close it (recurred on Lint Policy job 95354812245),
+  #748's capture-seam fix did not close it either (this instance is post-#748). Each recurrence
+  costs a full lane timeout budget and an operator cancel + same-SHA rerun.
+- Evidence: run 32080950731 attempt 1, job 95543690619 (Docgen, cancelled); log tail captured
+  before the rerun superseded the attempt.
+- What would have prevented the *cost* (independent of the root fix): the docgen stall watchdog
+  already probes sub-steps locally — a hosted-lane variant that fires when a wrapper phase
+  ("aggregating…") exceeds a generous multiple of its local duration would have converted a
+  54-minute operator-cancelled hang into a ~2-minute self-terminating red with the phase named.
+  Observability first: the fix for the hang is still open, but every instance should at least
+  self-report its stall point.
